  "narrative": "The new validation evidence confirms the two remaining high-value in-dataset associations. For H18, KEAP1 loss versus NFE2L2 dependency is registered in the direction of weaker NFE2L2 dependency; the independent validation signed estimate is +0.781, with interval +0.752 to +0.809. The 10% practical threshold for NFE2L2 is about 0.0093, so the validation interval is entirely and far above that threshold. Combined with the discovery estimate +0.796, this strongly supports KEAP1-loss cells having much weaker NFE2L2 dependency in this supplied dataset. I therefore accept and close H18. For H13, MSI-high versus WRN dependency is registered in the direction of stronger WRN dependency, i.e. a more negative WRN score. The newly delivered overall validation signed estimate is +0.771, interval +0.720 to +0.822. The WRN 10% threshold is about 0.0301, so the independent interval is also entirely and overwhelmingly above the practical threshold. This validates the overall MSI-high/WRN association, not only the adenocarcinoma subgroup result already validated in H24. I therefore accept and close H13. The core validated set is now SMARCA4 loss/stronger SMARCA2 dependency, TP53 loss/weaker USP7 dependency, KEAP1 loss/weaker NFE2L2 dependency, and MSI-high/stronger WRN dependency overall and in adenocarcinoma. The most informative unresolved signal is the NF1-loss bypass signal for KRAS dependency. The discovery result in KRAS-wildtype cells was +0.0387, interval +0.0062 to +0.0712, exceeding the KRAS threshold at the point estimate and excluding zero, but the lower bound is below the 10% practical threshold. I keep H67 unresolved and prioritize its registered context refinements plus two new analyses: a specificity interaction to test whether the NF1-loss effect is larger in KRAS-wildtype than KRAS-mutant cells, and a direct KRAS-mutation test in NF1-intact cells to see whether the borderline KRAS effect becomes meaningful when NF1-loss confounding is reduced. The borderline KRAS and EGFR main effects remain unresolved but are deferred rather than accepted; their existing intervals remain compatible with small effects crossing zero and threshold. The TP53-loss/MDM2 effect also remains unresolved but deferred, because the point estimate is small and the confidence interval only partly overlaps the practical threshold. These statements are associations within the supplied dataset and not claims of causality or external generalizability.",
  "research_notes": "Validated accepted core associations after synthesis: H7 SMARCA4 loss -> much stronger SMARCA2 dependency; H12 TP53 loss -> much weaker USP7 dependency; H18 KEAP1 loss -> much weaker NFE2L2 dependency; H13 MSI-high -> much stronger WRN dependency overall, and H24 validates adenocarcinoma. Conditional robustness checks for these core effects have been broadly supportive, so these main effect investigations are closed. Practical thresholds: SMARCA2 about 0.0415, WRN about 0.0301, NFE2L2/USP7 about 0.0093, KRAS about 0.0245, EGFR about 0.0250. Current priority: resolve NF1-loss effect on KRAS dependency. H67 in KRAS-wildtype cells: +0.0387 CI +0.0062 to +0.0712; positive and above zero but lower bound below the 10% threshold, so keep unresolved. Next high-value analyses: H68-H70 test TP53-intact, EGFR-amplification-intact, and adenocarcinoma contexts; new H71 interaction tests specificity versus KRAS-mutant cells using signed direction -1 so positive means NF1-loss effect is larger in KRAS-wildtype cells; new H72 re-tests direct KRAS mutation in NF1-intact cells. If these are consistently positive with lower confidence bounds near or above the KRAS threshold, consider validation; if not, close as unresolved borderline association. Borderline main KRAS and EGFR oncogene effects remain deferred, not accepted. TP53/MDM2 remains deferred unresolved. All conclusions remain in-dataset associations, not causal or generalizable claims."
